<BlogPost slug="copay-vs-coinsurance-what-you-will-actually-pay-for-treatment" toc={toc}>
  <p>In health insurance, &ldquo;copay&rdquo; refers to the set amount you&rsquo;ll pay for a service, like $30 for an office visit. &ldquo;Coinsurance&rdquo; refers to a percentage you pay, for example 25%, usually after you meet your deductible. Each works a little differently, but both contribute to your annual out-of-pocket maximum.</p>
  <p>If you or someone you love needs treatment for addiction, the last thing you need is to be stressed about payment definitions. Having a clear understanding of what terms like &ldquo;copay&rdquo; and &ldquo;coinsurance&rdquo; mean can help you navigate the admissions process more easily and get to care faster. In one focus-group study of people with opioid use disorder, 27 of 28 participants &mdash; 96.4% &mdash; said a financial barrier had delayed when they started treatment or made it harder to stay in it [1]. Understanding these terms ahead of time is one way to keep that from happening to you.</p>
  <p>Don&rsquo;t worry if you don&rsquo;t immediately recognize the terminology or understand insurance processes &mdash; our <a href="/admissions/">admissions team</a> is here to make things simple and walk you through it, however you need.</p>

  <figure class="bp-infographic">
