  <h2 id="what-is-copay">What Does &ldquo;Copay&rdquo; Mean?</h2>
  <p>&ldquo;Copay&rdquo; is short for copayment, a fixed price for a specific service you receive while in addiction treatment. These can include the price of medication prescriptions or accommodation if you&rsquo;re in a residential program. Your insurance plan sets the amount, and the plan pays the rest of the allowed cost. Residential and inpatient care is more often billed through your deductible and coinsurance, though some plans use a per-day or per-admission copay instead. Costs depend on your specific plan and whether the facility is in- or out-of-network.</p>
  <p>Copayments typically don&rsquo;t count toward your deductible. Your deductible is the amount you pay for certain covered services before your plan starts sharing those costs. Some services, like office visits or prescriptions, may carry a copay and be covered before you meet your deductible. Others, like inpatient or residential care, are often billed against your deductible first.</p>
  <p>One of the biggest benefits of a copay is that you have a consistent, upfront price for routine services, which makes financial planning more predictable.</p>

