  <h2 id="what-is-coinsurance">What Does &ldquo;Coinsurance&rdquo; Mean?</h2>
  <p>Coinsurance is the percentage you pay for addiction treatment once you&rsquo;ve reached your deductible. Your health insurance pays part of the cost, and you pay the rest. The percentage stays the same regardless of the specific service and is often written as a ratio in your policy &mdash; for example, 75/25 means your insurance provider pays 75% of the cost and you pay the remaining 25%.</p>
  <p>Because coinsurance only takes effect once you&rsquo;ve met your deductible, it can&rsquo;t contribute toward it. Coinsurance also stops once you hit your out-of-pocket maximum, the most you pay out of your own money for covered services before your insurance pays 100% for the rest of the plan year. This matters for detox and residential care, where costs can reach that cap.</p>

  <figure class="bp-infographic">
    <img src="/images/blog/infographics/coinsurance-worked-example.svg" alt="Example: after a $2,000 deductible, a $9,000 remaining treatment bill is split 75/25 coinsurance, so the insurance provider pays $6,750 and the patient pays $2,250. Both amounts count toward the out-of-pocket maximum." loading="lazy" width="760" height="400" />
    <figcaption>A worked example: 75/25 coinsurance on a $9,000 bill, after the deductible is already met.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Here&rsquo;s how that plays out: say you&rsquo;ve already met your $2,000 deductible, and your remaining addiction treatment costs $9,000. With 75/25 coinsurance, your provider pays $6,750 (75% of $9,000) and you pay $2,250 (25% of $9,000).</p>

  <h2 id="out-of-pocket">Do Copay and Coinsurance Count as Paying Out-of-Pocket?</h2>
  <p>Yes &mdash; both copayments and coinsurance are out-of-pocket payments, meaning they come from your own money, not your insurance provider&rsquo;s. It&rsquo;s important to note that both copay and coinsurance count toward your out-of-pocket maximum.</p>
  <p>For example, if your out-of-pocket maximum is $10,000 and your share of costs would have otherwise been $11,500, you pay $10,000 and your plan covers the remaining $1,500. Premiums, out-of-network care in some plans, and services your plan doesn&rsquo;t cover usually don&rsquo;t count toward that maximum.</p>

  <h2 id="all-providers">Do All Insurance Providers Offer Copay and Coinsurance?</h2>
  <p>No &mdash; health insurance providers aren&rsquo;t obligated to offer copay and coinsurance as part of their coverage. Many policies do integrate them, but it&rsquo;s best to check with your provider before you seek addiction treatment.</p>
  <p>It&rsquo;s also worth checking whether the addiction center and program you want to join is &ldquo;in-network&rdquo; with your insurance provider, meaning they have a contract and pre-negotiated rates &mdash; or &ldquo;out-of-network,&rdquo; which can still be well covered under many PPO plans, just at a different rate or through a different approval process. <a href="/insurance/">Learn how in-network and out-of-network coverage works</a> before you commit to a program.</p>

  <h2 id="avoid-surprises">How to Avoid Surprise Costs in Addiction Treatment</h2>
  <p>According to the National Center for Drug Abuse Statistics, the average cost of drug rehabilitation per person is $13,475. A 7-day detox stay can run from $1,750, and an Intensive Outpatient Program (IOP) has averaged around $4,939 in 2022 dollars [2]. Keep in mind these are just reference points, not what you&rsquo;ll necessarily pay &mdash; to keep your own treatment costs as transparent as possible, follow these steps:</p>

  <figure class="bp-infographic">
    <img src="/images/blog/infographics/avoid-surprise-treatment-costs-checklist.svg" alt="Six steps to avoid surprise addiction treatment costs: verify your insurance is in-network, request a full cost breakdown, know your deductible, confirm whether copay or coinsurance applies per service, keep records of every payment, and ask about payment plans." loading="lazy" width="760" height="560" />
    <figcaption>Six steps that keep costs predictable before you start treatment.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Verify insurance before starting treatment.</b> Check that your insurance provider is in- or out-of-network at the addiction center you want to attend, then contact the center to <a href="/admissions/verify-insurance/">verify your benefits</a> and get a rundown of costs for the services you need.</li>
    <li><b>Request a breakdown of costs.</b> Credible treatment centers will happily give you this information, including the cost of the initial assessment, medical detox, therapy sessions, and medication. Resistance or withholding is a red flag that the center isn&rsquo;t trustworthy.</li>
    <li><b>Know your deductible.</b> Contact your insurance provider and confirm your deductible amount, how much you&rsquo;ve already spent, and whether the percentage you pay for treatment changes if you haven&rsquo;t met it yet.</li>
    <li><b>Check whether copay or coinsurance applies to specific services.</b> Eligibility can change depending on the type of service, so confirm with your insurance provider for the particular services you want to receive.</li>
    <li><b>Keep records of payments.</b> Save estimates, payment receipts, and communication with your insurance provider and the addiction center &mdash; useful if there are any discrepancies or unexpected costs.</li>
    <li><b>Ask about payment plans and financial support.</b> Some treatment centers may offer sliding scales or alternative financing, such as incremental payments, to support people seeking addiction care.</li>
  </ul>

